in this lesson we're going to talk about  how to work with vectors so what is a  vector well a vector is oftentimes you  know drawn as an arrow  or like a ray and basically what it does  it has initial point a starting point  and a terminal point where it terminates  or stops and a vector basically has a  magnitude which is represented by the  length  and then direction which is represented  by the angle so if you were to have  let's say a longer  ray here a longer vector that would  represent like a stronger force for  example  and if it's pointing this way versus  this way that's just indicating the  direction that the force is exerted  so when you calculate the component form  of a vector what you do is you take the  terminal point minus the initial point  so the starting point  i'm sorry the ending point minus the  starting point right so terminal minus  initial so in this example if we're  going from 5 3 to 6 7 you would just  take the ending point 6 minus 5  okay so i'm subtracting the x  coordinates and then seven minus three  so terminal minus initial so that comes  out to one  four  so that means that you're going uh one  to the right and four up okay so this is  the horizontal component that's the  vertical component x and y  so it's different from slope where you  do y2 minus y1 over x2 minus x1 here  you're just subtracting the x  coordinates to give you the horizontal  direction  okay and distance and you're subtracting  the y coordinates to give you the  vertical direction and distance so  basically you just take terminal minus  initial now if you wanted to write this  vector instead of in component form in  what they call unit vector notation  what you do is you write this as 1 i  plus 4j what i represents is a unit  vector meaning a vector that's only one  unit long okay going in the x direction  or horizontal direction and then the j  represents a unit vector meaning one  unit going in the vertical direction so  this is one i mean you're going one to  the right and four up so if we were to  represent this like this you're going  right one up four so there's your vector  right there  okay now you can add vectors subtract  them and multiply them et cetera so  let's just look at some examples if  you're adding these two vectors all you  have to do is add the x components  that's the horizontal components and the  y components so in this case 3 plus 2 is  going to give us 5  and 5 plus 1 is going to give us 6. so  this these two vectors added together  give us 5 6. that's that's the new  vector when you add vectors okay not  just using the component form but just  looking at vectors in general what you  want is where one vector ends  you want the other vector to begin and  you can take a vector and you can slide  it left and right you can slide it up  and down you just don't want to rotate  it and you don't want to stretch it or  compress it you want to keep it the same  size but you can translate it so if i  translate this vector here  in such a way that where one ends the  other one begins sometimes  is referred to as you know head to tail  head to tail like that where one ends  the other one begins the end result or  the resultant vector would be this one  right here so instead of going like this  and like this the end result is going on  this diagonal from the beginning to the  end  that's called the resultant vector now  here when you multiply vectors what you  can do or i should say this is a scalar  multiple you're taking this vector  negative 1 5  and you're multiplying it by 4 you're  making it 4 times  longer and it's still going to go in the  same direction it's just going to be  magnified by four times so here you just  distribute the four to the x and y  components the horizontal components and  the vertical components so that's going  to be negative 420 and that's going to  be  scaled by four times so multiply by a  scalar of four so let's look at a couple  examples see if you can do these on your  own and we'll go through them so  example one says what is the unit vector  notation of vector ef so that's from  here to here so this is  the initial point this is the terminal  point  okay so what you can do is you can  either do the method we were doing here  where we were taking terminal minus  initial or you could go like this you  could make this into a little triangle  and you can see we're going so many  units to the right so many units up okay  but i'm going to use the terminal minus  initial so 3 minus negative 3  and negative 1  minus negative 2. so when you subtract  that's like adding the opposite that's 6  that's going to give you 1. so that's  going to be 6 1 so you're going right 6  up 1. so that's your that's your vector  now in unit vector notation this would  be written as 6 i  plus  1 j or you could just say plus j  that's the unit vector notation  horizontal and vertical  okay now for example two given vector u  and v such that u equals negative one  four and v equals four negative six what  is three u minus two v well here you can  see we're just scaling the vectors okay  three times u is just going to be what  it's going to be negative 3 comma 12  okay minus 2 times v so 2 times v would  be 8  negative 12 and we're subtracting so  negative 3 minus 8 is negative 11 and 12  minus negative 12 is  like adding 12 so that's going to be 24.  so  that's an overview of working with  vectors the component form the  unit vector notation  multiplying adding subtracting and so  forth review this lesson if you need to  and i'll see you in the next lesson

